.TH CVB 1 "October 2026" "CVB 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cvb - terminal-to-LLM exchange utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cvb
.RI [ COMMAND ]
.br
.B cvb on
.br
.B cvb off
.br
.B cvb
.I path
.br
.B cvb
.I command
.RI [ args... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B CVB
(Capture (-> View (-> Bridge) is a terminal utility for reliable exchange between a human, the terminal, the clipboard, and an LLM.

CVB can capture completed commands and their terminal output in a resident Bash session, capture the currently visible terminal screen with a configurable hotkey, bridge files to the clipboard, recursively inventory directories as TSV, and execute external commands.

CVB is not a shell replacement, terminal logger, terminal database, or AI agent.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B on
Start a resident CVB Bash session.

Commands entered in the session are captured when they complete. Their visible terminal output is copied to the Wayland clipboard.

The active session is indicated by a red dot in the shell prompt.
.TP
.B off
End the active CVB Bash session.

When used outside an active session, CVB reports that no session is running.
.TP
.B --help, -h
Display command-line help.
.TP
.B --version, -V
Display the CVB version.
.TP
.I path
If the argument names an existing filesystem path, bridge it according to its type.

Regular files are copied to the clipboard unchanged. Directories are recursively represented as TSV.
.TP
.I command
If the first argument does not name an existing filesystem path, execute it as an external command with the remaining arguments.

Common terminal pagers are disabled while executing commands so their output can be captured directly.
.SH RESIDENT CAPTURE
The resident mode is normally started through the Bash wrapper supplied with CVB.

Source the wrapper into the parent Bash shell before using
.B cvb on.
.PP
The wrapper creates a temporary shell-state file and starts a child Bash session. Useful parent-shell state is transferred to the child, including aliases, functions, shell options, shopt settings, and inherited environment variables.
.PP
CVB owns the capture-related prompt and shell hooks while the session is active. The parent shell is restored when the child session exits. Commands entered during the session are added to the parent shell's history.
.SH SCREEN CAPTURE
While a resident CVB session is running, CVB maintains a representation of the terminal's currently visible screen.
.PP
The default screen-capture binding is:
.PP
.EX
Ctrl+Shift+Space
.EE
.PP
Pressing the binding copies the current visible terminal screen to the Wayland clipboard.
.PP
Screen capture works with both ordinary shell sessions and full-screen interactive terminal applications such as
.B htop
and
.BR micro .
The captured text represents the current visible terminal screen rather than command history or terminal scrollback.
.PP
The screen-capture binding is configured through:
.PP
.EX
~/.config/cvb/keybind
.EE
.PP
If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set, the file is instead located at
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cvb/keybind .
If the file does not exist, CVB creates it with the default binding.
.PP
Each non-empty, non-comment line defines one binding. Multiple bindings are allowed.
.PP
Supported modifiers are
.BR Ctrl ,
.BR Shift ,
.BR Alt ,
and
.BR Super .
The key is separated from modifiers with
.BR + .
.PP
Named special keys include
.BR Enter ,
.BR Escape ,
.BR Tab ,
.BR Space ,
.BR Backspace ,
.BR Delete ,
.BR Insert ,
.BR Home ,
.BR End ,
.BR PageUp ,
.BR PageDown ,
.BR Left ,
.BR Right ,
.BR Up ,
.BR Down ,
and
.BR F1
through
.BR F24 .
.PP
Named punctuation keys include
.BR Plus ,
.BR Minus ,
.BR Comma ,
.BR Period ,
.BR Slash ,
.BR Backslash ,
.BR Semicolon ,
.BR Apostrophe ,
.BR LeftBracket ,
.BR RightBracket ,
and
.BR Backtick .
.PP
The configuration is watched while CVB is running. Changes take effect without restarting the resident session.
.PP
CVB observes configured key combinations but does not consume the underlying keyboard events. The terminal or application may therefore also receive the key combination.
.SH FILE BRIDGING
An existing filesystem path takes precedence over an external command.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.EX
cvb /path/to/file.txt
.EE
.PP
copies the complete contents of the file to the Wayland clipboard.
.SH DIRECTORY BRIDGING
Directories are recursively represented as tab-separated values.
.PP
The output has the following form:
.PP
.EX
path	type	size
one.txt	file	4
subdir	dir	-
subdir/two.txt	file	4
.EE
.PP
Paths are relative to the selected directory. Regular files report their byte size and directories use
.BR - .
Symlinks and other filesystem object types are skipped.
.SH IGNORE LIST
CVB creates
.I ~/.config/cvb/ignore
on first directory inventory. If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set, the file is instead located at
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cvb/ignore .
.PP
The file contains one directory name per line. Blank lines and lines beginning with
.B #
are ignored. A directory name is excluded wherever it occurs in the directory tree.
.PP
The initial file excludes common version-control directories:
.PP
.EX
.git
.hg
.svn
.bzr
.jj
.EE
.PP
The file is the complete source of truth: remove an entry to include that directory, or add entries such as
.B target
or
.B node_modules
to exclude them.
.PP
CVB does not interpret glob patterns, regular expressions, or
.BR .gitignore
syntax.
.SH CLIPBOARD
CVB uses
.B wl-copy
for Wayland clipboard integration.
.PP
When a captured command produces no visible terminal output, CVB copies the marker
.BR "[NO_VISIBLE_OUTPUT]" .
.PP
Commands listed in the skip list are an exception: when they produce no visible output the clipboard is left unchanged.
Output, including error messages, is captured as usual.
.PP
Screen captures copy the textual contents of the currently visible terminal screen.
.SH SKIP LIST
CVB creates
.I ~/.config/cvb/skip
when a resident session starts. If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set, the file is instead located at
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cvb/skip .
.PP
The file contains one command name per line. Blank lines and text after
.B #
are ignored. The initial file lists
.BR cd .
.PP
A command line is skipped only when every top-level command in it is listed, matched by its first word, and the line produced no visible output.
Commands inside functions and compound commands such as
.B if
are not matched.
The file is read on every command, so changes apply immediately. Screen captures are not affected.
.SH TARGET RESOLUTION
Existing filesystem paths take precedence over external commands.
.PP
For example, if
.B Cargo.toml
exists in the current directory,
.B cvb Cargo.toml
bridges the file rather than attempting to execute a command named
.BR Cargo.toml .
.PP
If the path does not exist, the arguments are treated as an external command.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/cvb
The CVB executable when installed system-wide.
.TP
.I /usr/share/cvb/cvb.bash
Bash wrapper providing the
.B cvb on
and
.B cvb off
commands.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/cvb/
Temporary CVB shell-state and Bash initialization files.
.TP
.I ~/.config/cvb/keybind
Screen-capture key bindings. If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set, the file is located at
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cvb/keybind .
.TP
.I ~/.config/cvb/ignore
Directory names excluded from directory inventories. If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set, the file is located at
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cvb/ignore .
.SH REQUIREMENTS
CVB requires:
.IP (bu 2
Linux
.IP (bu 2
Bash
.IP (bu 2
Wayland
.IP (bu 2
wl-clipboard
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested operation completed successfully.
.TP
.B non-zero
The operation failed, or an executed external command returned a non-zero status.
